card.statement:
[<<cite:Ha06>>]
Suppose $V$ has no proper inner breaks. If $\{v_i\}_{i\in J}$ is a $\K$-basis for $V_\mf$ ($J$ some index set), then the following is a $\K$-basis for $V$:

\begin{equation}
C:=\{a_g v_i\mid g\in S, i\in J\}
\end{equation}

where $S\subseteq\tilde G_\mf$ is a set of representatives for $\tilde G_\mf$ modulo $G_\mf$.
Moreover,
for any $v\in V_\mf$, any $i\in\{1,\ldots,n\}$ and $g\in S$ we have

\begin{equation}
X_ia_g v = \begin{cases} a_h b_{g,i} v,& g+e_i\in\tilde G_{\mf}\\
0,&\text{otherwise}, \end{cases}
\qquad
Y_ia_g v = \begin{cases} a_k c_{g,i} v,& g-e_i\in\tilde G_{\mf}\\
0,&\text{otherwise}. \end{cases}
\end{equation}

where $h,k\in S$ with $h\in (g+e_i)+ G_\mf$ and $k\in (g-e_i)+G_\mf$
and $b_{g,i},c_{g,i}\in B(\mf)$ are given by

\begin{equation}
b_{g,i}=\si_{-h}(X_ia_ga_{g+e_i-h}'a_h')a_{g+e_i-h},
\qquad
c_{g,i}=\si_{-k}(Y_ia_ga_{g-e_i-k}'a_k')a_{g-e_i-k}.
\end{equation}
